-- title:
--   §C.1, $n=3$ — exchange gives $U(1)$ on $\mathrm{DFS}_3(\tfrac32)$ alone and $SU(2)$ on $\mathrm{DFS}_3(\tfrac12)$ alone
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be the real Lie algebra generated by $iE_{12},iE_{13},iE_{23}$ on three qubits, and let $W_{3/2}$, $W_{1/2}$ be the total-spin-$\tfrac32$ and $\tfrac12$ subspaces.
--
--   1. $L$ contains an operator that acts as $i\,I$ on $W_{3/2}$ and as $0$ on its orthogonal complement; so $L$ contains $\mathfrak u(1)=i\mathbb R$ acting on $\mathrm{DFS}_3(\tfrac32)$ alone.
--   2. Every traceless anti-Hermitian $X$ that commutes with $S^{[3]}_x,S^{[3]}_y,S^{[3]}_z$ and annihilates the orthogonal complement of $W_{1/2}$ lies in $L$.
--
--   An operator commuting with the collective spins and supported on $W_{1/2}\cong\mathbb C^2\otimes\mathbb C^2$ has the form $Y\otimes I_2$, with $\operatorname{tr}X=2\operatorname{tr}Y$; so clause 2 says that $L$ contains $\mathfrak{su}(2)\otimes I_2$ acting on $\mathrm{DFS}_3(\tfrac12)$ and as zero elsewhere. This is the base case $n=3$ of the induction of Appendix C.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $L$ is `exchangeLie 3`; Hamiltonians $H$ are represented by $iH$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: "we can perform U(1) on DFS3(3/2) alone" → L contains an element acting as i·I on the total-spin-3/2 subspace and 0 on its complement; reason: that element spans u(1) on that block; the unitaries are its exponentials
--   - reading: "SU(2) on DFS3(1/2) alone" (gates, by an Euler angle construction) → every element of su(2) ⊗ I₂ on the total-spin-1/2 block, extended by zero, lies in L; reason: the connected group of such gates has exactly this Lie algebra, and the page obtains the gates from the Lie algebra elements σ_z, σ_x of (C.4)
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, §C.1, after (C.4), p.246 (PDF p.263)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_collS
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_exchangeLie
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_dfsSpace
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_suOn

open scoped Matrix

attribute [local instance 100] LieRing.ofAssociativeRing

namespace BaconThesis.ExchangeUniv

/-- **§C.1, `n = 3`: `U(1)` on `DFS₃(3/2)` alone and `SU(2)` on `DFS₃(1/2)` alone.**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, §C.1, after (C.4), p.246 (PDF p.263): "it is possible to act independently on DFS₃(3/2)
and DFS₃(1/2). In other words, we can perform U(1) on DFS₃(3/2) alone, and SU(2) on DFS₃(1/2)
alone."

1. The BaconThesis.Collective.exchange Lie algebra `L` of three qubits contains an element acting as `i·I` on the
   total-spin-`3/2` subspace and as `0` on its orthogonal complement (so `L` contains
   `u(1) = iℝ` on `DFS₃(3/2)` alone).
2. Every traceless anti-Hermitian `X` commuting with `S_x, S_y, S_z`, acting only on the
   total-spin-`1/2` subspace (zero on its orthogonal complement), lies in `L`: this is
   `su(2) ⊗ I₂` on `DFS₃(1/2)` alone.

**Formalization Note.** `L = exchangeLie 3`, the real Lie span of the `i·E_ij`; an operator
commuting with every `S_α` and supported on the spin-`1/2` block is `Y ⊗ I₂` in the block, and
`tr X = 2 tr Y`, so clause 2 is exactly `su(2) ⊗ I₂` extended by zero. -/
theorem n3_independent :
    (∃ X ∈ exchangeLie 3,
      (∀ v ∈ dfsSpace 3 (3 / 2), X *ᵥ v = Complex.I • v) ∧
      (∀ v : (Fin 3 → Fin 2) → ℂ, (∀ w ∈ dfsSpace 3 (3 / 2), star w ⬝ᵥ v = 0) → X *ᵥ v = 0)) ∧
    (∀ X : Matrix (Fin 3 → Fin 2) (Fin 3 → Fin 2) ℂ,
      X ∈ suOn (dfsSpace 3 (1 / 2)) → (∀ α : Fin 3, Commute X (collS 3 α)) →
        X ∈ exchangeLie 3) := by sorry

end BaconThesis.ExchangeUniv
